On your two questions:

1. **CLAUDE.md reading** —yes, both are injected automatically at the start of every conversation, before I do anything else. This turn I received the global `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (statelessness, no cross-project memory) and this repo's `CLAUDE.md` (the iteration workflow) both verbatim, labeled "IMPORTANT... OVERRIDE any default behavior." This isn't something I opt into —the harness assembles it and hands it to me at session start regardless of what I'd otherwise do. So if your strategy depends on that always happening, it's working: I have not written anything to the persistent memory system this session, in compliance with your global file's "do not rely on or create cross-project memory" instruction. I don't have visibility into the rest of what's under `~/.claude` (hooks, settings, etc.) beyond the CLAUDE.md text itself, so I can't vouch for "everything" —just confirming the file-reading mechanism you're asking about is intact.

2. **Cloudflare caching** —yes, legitimately a real worry: your site has no cache-busting in its filenames (`main.js`, `styles.css`, `content.*.js` keep the same name release to release), so both the browser's disk cache and Cloudflare's edge cache can keep serving the old bytes under those exact URLs after you upload new ones, until whatever TTL is set expires. Two ways to close it, simplest first: **manual purge on every deploy** —Cloudflare dashboard → Caching → Configuration → "Purge Everything" (or "Custom Purge" with just the changed URLs), one click, zero setup, reliable, good fit for infrequent deploys; or **a Cache Rule** —Caching → Cache Rules, a rule for your HTML/JS/CSS paths setting Cache Level to "Bypass" (or Edge Cache TTL to "no-cache"), so Cloudflare always revalidates with origin instead of serving from edge cache blindly, set once, no manual step per deploy, at the cost of a little more origin traffic. If deploying through Cloudflare Pages specifically rather than Cloudflare as a proxy in front of another host, this is largely a non-issue —Pages invalidates its own cache automatically on every new deploy.
